---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const breadcrumbs = [
{ label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' },
{ label: 'Pet Exercise by Age', url: '/en/blog/pet-exercise-by-age/' }
];
const siteUrl = "https://www.petaginghub.com";
const hrefLangAlternates = [
{ lang: 'zh-TW', url: siteUrl + '/blog/pet-exercise-by-age' },
{ lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/pet-exercise-by-age' },
{ lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/blog/pet-exercise-by-age' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Pet Exercise Guide by Age - Pet Aging Hub"
description="Age-appropriate activity guides for dogs and cats at every life stage. Too little or too much exercise both cause problems."
lang="en" breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}>
<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4">
<header class="mb-8">
<span class="inline-block bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Wellness</span>
<h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">Age-Appropriate Exercise for Dogs and Cats</h1>
<p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Adjust activity gradually to the individual pet’s fitness, breed, age, health and response. Do not force movement through pain or marked fatigue. Shorter, gentler activity may suit some older pets; discuss new or meaningful mobility changes with your veterinarian.</p>
</header>
<div class="space-y-10">
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-5">Dogs: Exercise by Life Stage</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<div class="flex justify-between items-start mb-2">
<h3 class="font-bold text-amber-800">Puppies</h3>
<span class="text-xs bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 px-2 py-1 rounded-full">Gradual, individual activity</span>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">For a puppy, choose activity appropriate to individual growth, fitness and health. Observe comfort and recovery rather than using a five-minutes-per-month rule. Do not force continued activity through pain or marked fatigue; discuss concerns with your veterinarian.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<div class="flex justify-between items-start mb-2">
<h3 class="font-bold text-amber-800">Adult dogs</h3>
<span class="text-xs bg-green-100 text-green-700 px-2 py-1 rounded-full">Adapt to fitness, health and response</span>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">For an adult dog, start from current fitness and adjust activity gradually. Breed, health, weather and response matter more than a universal daily minute target. Stop forcing activity through pain or marked fatigue. <a href="/en/blog/pet-nutrition-by-age/" class="text-blue-600 underline">pet nutrition for your dog's age</a></p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<div class="flex justify-between items-start mb-2">
<h3 class="font-bold text-amber-800">Older dogs</h3>
<span class="text-xs bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 px-2 py-1 rounded-full">Gentler activity where appropriate</span>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Some older dogs may suit shorter, gentler walks or accessible scent games. Observe comfort and recovery, and discuss new stiffness or limping rather than dismissing it as age. <a href="/en/blog/pet-joint-health/" class="text-blue-600 underline">pet joint health guide</a> · <a href="/en/blog/senior-dog-care/" class="text-blue-600 underline">senior dog care guide</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-5">Cats: Activity by Life Stage</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<div class="bg-purple-50 rounded-2xl p-5 border border-purple-100">
<h3 class="font-bold text-purple-800 mb-2">Kittens</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Offer interactive play and accessible exploration suited to your cat’s interests and ability. Adjust sessions to the response rather than a fixed number of minutes. New reluctance to move, reduced jumping or discomfort warrants veterinary discussion.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-purple-50 rounded-2xl p-5 border border-purple-100">
<h3 class="font-bold text-purple-800 mb-2">Adult cats</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Offer interactive play and accessible exploration suited to your cat’s interests and ability. Adjust sessions to the response rather than a fixed number of minutes. New reluctance to move, reduced jumping or discomfort warrants veterinary discussion.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-purple-50 rounded-2xl p-5 border border-purple-100">
<h3 class="font-bold text-purple-800 mb-2">Older cats</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Make preferred play and resting places accessible for an older cat. Gentle play may suit some cats, but new difficulty jumping, grooming or using the litter box needs assessment rather than a fixed exercise prescription.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Best Low-Impact Activities for Senior Pets</h2>
<div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
<div class="bg-amber-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-amber-100">
<h3 class="font-bold text-amber-800 mb-2">Senior Dogs</h3>
<ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-1">
<li>Gentle leash walks on flat surfaces</li>
<li>Swimming or rehabilitation only when assessed as suitable by a veterinarian</li>
<li>Nose work (scent games)</li>
<li>Short training sessions</li>
<li>Slow-paced fetch on soft grass</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="bg-purple-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-purple-100">
<h3 class="font-bold text-purple-800 mb-2">Senior Cats</h3>
<ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-1">
<li>Slow wand toy sessions</li>
<li>Puzzle feeders</li>
<li>Cat TV and bird watching setups</li>
<li>Gentle grooming sessions</li>
<li>Hiding treats for searching</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</div>
<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
<a href="/en/blog/pet-joint-health/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Joint Health Guide</a>
<a href="/en/blog/senior-pet-health-guide/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-slate-100 hover:bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Senior Pet Guide</a>
</div>
</article>

<p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-600">Source: <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/pain-management/" class="underline">AAHA individualized pain and mobility management</a>. This supports veterinary assessment and tailored care, not a universal minute-based exercise prescription.</p>
<section class="mt-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><details class="mb-3"><summary>How much exercise does a senior dog need?</summary><p class="mt-2">Adjust activity gradually to the individual pet’s fitness, breed, age, health and response. Do not force movement through pain or marked fatigue. Shorter, gentler activity may suit some older pets; discuss new or meaningful mobility changes with your veterinarian.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>Can you over-exercise a puppy?</summary><p class="mt-2">For a puppy, choose activity appropriate to individual growth, fitness and health. Observe comfort and recovery rather than using a five-minutes-per-month rule. Do not force continued activity through pain or marked fatigue; discuss concerns with your veterinarian.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>How do I keep my indoor cat active?</summary><p class="mt-2">Offer interactive play and accessible exploration suited to your cat’s interests and ability. Adjust sessions to the response rather than a fixed number of minutes. New reluctance to move, reduced jumping or discomfort warrants veterinary discussion.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>What low-impact activities are safe for senior dogs?</summary><p class="mt-2">Gentle walks, scent games or accessible play may suit some dogs. Swimming or rehabilitation is not suitable for every patient and should be discussed with a veterinarian. Monitor the response; stop forcing activity through pain or marked fatigue.</p></details></section>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
"@context": "https://schema.org",
"@type": "FAQPage",
"mainEntity": [
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "How much exercise does a senior dog need?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Adjust activity gradually to the individual pet’s fitness, breed, age, health and response. Do not force movement through pain or marked fatigue. Shorter, gentler activity may suit some older pets; discuss new or meaningful mobility changes with your veterinarian."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Can you over-exercise a puppy?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "For a puppy, choose activity appropriate to individual growth, fitness and health. Observe comfort and recovery rather than using a five-minutes-per-month rule. Do not force continued activity through pain or marked fatigue; discuss concerns with your veterinarian."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "How do I keep my indoor cat active?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Offer interactive play and accessible exploration suited to your cat’s interests and ability. Adjust sessions to the response rather than a fixed number of minutes. New reluctance to move, reduced jumping or discomfort warrants veterinary discussion."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What low-impact activities are safe for senior dogs?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Gentle walks, scent games or accessible play may suit some dogs. Swimming or rehabilitation is not suitable for every patient and should be discussed with a veterinarian. Monitor the response; stop forcing activity through pain or marked fatigue."
}
}
]
})} />

</BaseLayout>
